---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const all = (await Promise.all([
  getCollection('research',   e => !e.data.draft),
  getCollection('analysis',   e => !e.data.draft),
  getCollection('build-logs', e => !e.data.draft),
])).flat();

function byThread(thread: string) {
  return all
    .filter(e => e.data.threads.includes(thread))
    .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
}

function lane(e: typeof all[0]) {
  if (e.collection === 'research')   return 'research';
  if (e.collection === 'analysis')   return 'analysis';
  return 'build-logs';
}

function fmt(d: Date) {
  return new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long' }).format(d);
}

const agentsArticles  = byThread('agents-as-people');
const intentArticles  = byThread('intent-consent');
const smallLargeArticles = byThread('small-vs-large');
---
<Base
  title="Manifesto — cyberdaemon.ai"
  description="What this is, what I got wrong, and what I'm trying to figure out."
>
  <div class="max-w-article mx-auto px-6 py-16">

    <header class="mb-14">
      <p class="lane-tag mb-4">cyberdaemon.ai</p>
      <h1 class="font-serif text-title text-text-primary mb-6 leading-tight" style="max-width: 580px; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        What this is
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">

      <p>
        I'm a cybersecurity practitioner who got tired of giving advice I couldn't verify.
        Enterprise architecture, identity, access control, Zero Trust, AI governance.
        I've had those conversations hundreds of times. I'm good at them. But there's a version
        of what I do that's well-credentialed guessing, and I knew it.
      </p>

      <p>
        So I built it. Prometheus is a production AI system running on a homelab cluster:
        multi-agent orchestration, episodic memory, runtime authorization, behavioral drift
        detection, autonomous build agents. Enterprise-class architecture on a small budget.
        The same patterns I'd spec for a client, running against my actual work.
      </p>

      <h2>What I got wrong</h2>

      <p>
        Two things I believed that turned out to be wrong.
      </p>

      <p>
        First: AI is just a chatbot. Better autocomplete. I was wrong not because the models
        are smarter than I thought, but because the architecture of building <em>with</em> AI
        is fundamentally different from anything else I'd built. The failure surface is completely
        different. The mental model of "model as API call" breaks the moment you try to run
        production agents.
      </p>

      <p>
        Second: I could build it with a bunch of scripts. I did. Then reality systematically
        replaced everything I'd built, and I understood what I'd been missing. I wasn't thinking
        in systems. I was writing procedures. Scripts execute procedures. Systems enforce intent.
        The gap between those two things is where most agent projects fail, including my early ones.
      </p>

      <h2 id="agents-as-people">Agents as something between a tool and a person</h2>

      <p>
        Prometheus has persistent memory, measurable behavioral consistency, and affect sensing.
        At some point the governance model built for "AI as software process" produces wrong designs.
        I don't know exactly where that point is. The question isn't whether the agent is conscious
        -- that's a distraction. The question is: at what level of persistence and consistency does
        the process model fail?
      </p>

      {agentsArticles.length > 0 && (
        <div class="related-thread">
          <p class="thread-label">Related</p>
          <ul>
            {agentsArticles.map(e => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${lane(e)}/${e.slug}`}>{e.data.title}</a>
                <span class="thread-date">{fmt(e.data.date)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <h2 id="intent-consent">Intent and consent</h2>

      <p>
        Right now, an agent's mission is imposed via a signed authorization token. The agent
        doesn't choose it. That's architecturally correct for current capability levels. But
        what would it mean for an agent to consent to a mission? Is that a useful design
        primitive, or a category error? I don't know. I'm not willing to pretend the question
        doesn't exist.
      </p>

      {intentArticles.length > 0 && (
        <div class="related-thread">
          <p class="thread-label">Related</p>
          <ul>
            {intentArticles.map(e => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${lane(e)}/${e.slug}`}>{e.data.title}</a>
                <span class="thread-date">{fmt(e.data.date)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <h2 id="small-vs-large">Small models versus large, and what you can actually know</h2>

      <p>
        Running local inference on hardware I own is different in kind from calling a cloud
        API, not just in cost. I can know things about a local model -- behavioral stability,
        drift patterns, response characteristics -- that are structurally unknowable about a
        hosted model that gets silently updated. That difference matters for how you design
        authorization and governance. Still working out exactly how.
      </p>

      {smallLargeArticles.length > 0 && (
        <div class="related-thread">
          <p class="thread-label">Related</p>
          <ul>
            {smallLargeArticles.map(e => (
              <li>
                <a href={`/${lane(e)}/${e.slug}`}>{e.data.title}</a>
                <span class="thread-date">{fmt(e.data.date)}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      )}

      <h2>What this site is</h2>

      <p>
        Where I document what I find. Research on problems I think the field is getting wrong.
        Analysis of frameworks that don't survive contact with deployed systems. Build logs for
        what Prometheus cost me to get there.
      </p>

      <p>
        Everything here is backed by a running system. Not a demo. Production, against real work.
        When I claim something, I can show you the receipts. When the system proves me wrong,
        I'll write about that too.
      </p>

      <p>
        My thinking changes. I'm working in the same fog as everyone else. I'll be honest when
        I update a position. I'm more interested in understanding things than being right about
        them, which sounds good and is genuinely uncomfortable in practice.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-border flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4">
      <p class="text-small text-text-muted font-sans">
        What I believe as of September 2026. This changes.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
        <a href="/research"   class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">Research →</a>
        <a href="/analysis"   class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">Analysis →</a>
        <a href="/build-logs" class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">Build Logs →</a>
        <a href="/about"      class="font-sans text-small text-text-muted hover:text-text-secondary transition-colors">About →</a>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .related-thread {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-border);
    background: var(--color-surface-secondary, rgba(255,255,255,0.03));
  }
  .thread-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .related-thread ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.375rem;
  }
  .related-thread li {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .related-thread a {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .related-thread a:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .thread-date {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
